A||||1||merlin||Compression check with top end apart?||||5||
Z||000000||merlin||11-04-2001||08:41 PM||||I’m in the process of installing my reconditioned heads on my 1987 V6 GT and I’m a little paranoid about the valve lash dilemma (1-1/2 turns, 3/4 turns, 1/2 turn, etc.)  Anyway, could I do a compression check with just the heads installed without anything else?  In the past I’ve had someone crank the engine from the driver’s seat while I tested compression.  How would I connect the battery up to the starter so that I can just crank the engine over?  I’m afraid of installing the battery and frying something, given all the electrical connections I’ve disconnected. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||tgowens||11-04-2001||09:11 PM||||You can connect the battery and use a remote starter. I generally start valve lash at 3/4 of a turn after snug. squirt about three squirts of oil in the spark plug hole of the cylinder you are compression checking, connect the compression gauge, crank the engine over five times and read the compression. Ideally the compression between cylinders should be + or - 10% of the other cylinders. If you get 0 compression, take a 1/4 turn off of the valve lash......<P>That's the way I do it.......<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>West Highland Graphics & Design</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
